[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known, with one fix queued. Read by what the bodies say, and by the tenant and form each
report came through, it holds more than one bug. Each problem below has its own ticket now. The
counts are built from the ticket export when the page renders.

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so
7 April comes out as 4 July. These reports come in by app, email and booking-v2 forms. This is the
bug #4471 names, and its queued fix covers this row only.

### `hint`: booking-v3 prints a month-first hint

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the tenant's way, day
first. People type what the hint tells them, and the form flips it. Only form reports from tenants
on booking-v3 land here. The parser fix does not reach them: the hint, or how the form reads it,
has to change.

### `one-day`: dates move by one day

These reports do not describe a flipped day and month. The date moves by one day: an invite one day
off, one meeting on Tuesday in Lisbon and on Monday in Toronto, an invite that arrived before the
booking was made. A day-first parser cannot do that; time-zone handling can. The desk listed them
in `desk-notes.json` and footnoted them under `parser`. They are a bug of their own. The counts
follow the desk: these reports stay in the `parser` count, where they are filed, and are listed again
here.

<Callout kind="warn">
Intake tags are not causes. Intake guessed <code>hint</code> from a US browser locale; the cause
depends on the tenant and its form. The "tagged as" column shows where the two disagree.
</Callout>

## What this changes at the desk

- Closing #4471 when the parser fix ships would leave the `hint` and `one-day` reports with no
  ticket. Track each problem on its own ticket.
- Subject lines did not separate these problems: the same subjects turn up under all three.
  Decision 0001 stays as accepted; a new decision record should replace it.

